You’re almost there – the only thing that stops the OSM raster tiles from lining up with the rest of the map is the **default tile size**.  
MapLibre assumes raster tiles are 512 px unless you tell it otherwise, and the public OSM slippy-map tiles are 256 px. Add `tileSize: 256` (and, optionally, the usual metadata) when you create the source, then add a raster layer that points at it.

```js
// 1️⃣ Add the source
map.addSource('osm-basemap', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: [
    'https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
  ],
  tileSize: 256,          // <-- required for correct zoom alignment
  minzoom: 0,             // optional, but makes the range explicit
  maxzoom: 19,            // OSM serves up to z-19
  attribution: '&copy; <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap</a> contributors'
});

// 2️⃣ Add a raster layer (insert it below the first symbol layer so you don’t cover labels)
const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol')?.id;
map.addLayer(
  {
    id: 'osm-basemap-layer',
    type: 'raster',
    source: 'osm-basemap',
    paint: {}
  },
  firstSymbol   // ← insert below labels; omit this argument to append on top
);
```

**What this does**

| Piece | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| `tileSize: 256` | Aligns the raster tiles with the map’s zoom levels (default 512 would make the imagery appear at the wrong scale). |
| `minzoom` / `maxzoom` | Gives MapLibre the correct zoom range; OSM tiles exist from 0 to 19. |
| `attribution` | Required by the OSM licence and shows up in the map’s UI. |
| Inserting the layer **before** the first `symbol` layer | Prevents the raster from covering street-name labels and other symbols. |

With those fields in place the OSM raster tiles will load, respect the map’s zoom, and coexist nicely with any vector layers you add later. No additional CORS handling is needed – the OSM tile server already sends the proper `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header.

