You’re almost there – the only thing you have to add for the standard OSM slippy-map tiles is the **`tileSize`** (and, optionally, some metadata that helps the style and other developers).

```js
// 1️⃣ Add the source
map.addSource('osm-basemap', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: [
    'https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
  ],
  // OSM tiles are 256 px, while the raster source default is 512 px
  tileSize: 256,

  // optional but recommended metadata
  minzoom: 0,
  maxzoom: 19,               // OSM’s published max-zoom
  attribution: '&copy; <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap</a> contributors'
});
```

```js
// 2️⃣ Add a raster layer that draws the source
map.addLayer({
  id: 'osm-basemap-layer',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'osm-basemap',
  // Insert it below the first symbol layer so you don’t cover labels
  // (find the first symbol layer id programmatically if you want it robust)
  // Example:
  //   before: map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol').id
});
```

### Why `tileSize: 256` matters
* The raster source defaults to `tileSize: 512`.  
* OSM’s tiles are 256 px, so without overriding the size the imagery will appear at the wrong zoom level (labels and other vector layers will be mis-aligned) and you’ll see a blurry or offset basemap even though the network requests succeed.

### Optional extras (good practice)

| Property | Why you might want it |
|----------|-----------------------|
| `minzoom` / `maxzoom` | Constrains the range MapLibre will request; prevents unnecessary 404s at zoom levels the server doesn’t serve. |
| `attribution` | Required by OSM’s tile-use policy and automatically shows up in the map’s attribution control. |
| `bounds` (if you want to limit the view) | Not required for OSM, but useful for custom tile sets. |

### CORS note
The OSM tile server already sends the proper `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header, so no extra CORS configuration is needed.

With the source defined as above and a raster layer added (preferably inserted **below the first `symbol` layer** so street names stay visible), the OSM tiles will render correctly.
